Description: Keep explicit-sync commits in order instead of dropping a frame not yet ready
 WlSurface::commit() with a linux-drm-syncobj acquire point waits for the
 fence before applying anything, and a commit arriving before that fence
 signalled superseded it: the waiting buffer was released unused and only
 the newest commit was kept. A client drawing faster than its GPU finishes
 (Vulkan MAILBOX or IMMEDIATE while GPU bound: Potluck on the Radeon 8060S,
 2026-11-27) always has its next frame committed by then, so every frame
 was superseded and the window kept showing an old one for as long as the
 game kept drawing. Clicks in its menu worked, but the screen only changed
 when focus left the window and the game slowed down.
 .
 Commits now queue in order, each with the state it had when committed
 (requests sent after a commit no longer fold into it, half of
 canonical/mir#5460), and apply as their fences signal. A waiting frame is
 dropped only once a newer one is ready, its other state (damage, frame
 callbacks, subsurface order) going with the newer one; a client 16 commits
 ahead has its oldest frame let go, as before. A commit without a fence
 waits behind fenced ones instead of overtaking them. Merging keeps the
 opaque region and surface specification, which WlSurfaceState::update_from()
 dropped (also for synchronized subsurfaces' cached commits), and a
 subsurface destroyed while an order is pending leaves that order.
Author: Umoja developers
Forwarded: not yet (bug report drafted in packaging/mir/upstream-bugs/)
Last-Update: 2026-11-27
